不同的原因，这也是一段非常令人不安的时期。但我认为你也可以说，这也是对英格兰和不列颠对民主原则承诺的肯定。因为我们已经看到了民主可以有多么动荡。我们经历了两次存在主义的全民公投。我们针对我们所生活的国家的组成进行了公投。我们又针对英国运作所处的、最重要的外交事务背景进行了公投。我们对这两者都进行了公投，并且我们都遵守了公投结果。而且，无论人们可能对议会制和简单多数票当选制（first past the post）提出多少批评，它确实促成了某些相当激进的

73
00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:53.000
kinds of reasons. But I think you could also say that it's been an affirmation of the commitment of England and Britain, perhaps more generally, to principles of democracy. Because what we've seen is how turbulent democracy can be. We have had two existential referendums. We've had one on the very makeup of the country that we live in. And we've had another on the most significant foreign affairs context within which Britain had operated. And we have had referendums on both, and we have abided by the results on both. And also, however much criticism one might level at a parliamentary system, and first past the post, it has facilitated the articulation of some quite kind of radical
立场的表达。所以无论是苏格兰的苏格兰民族党，还是奈杰尔·法拉奇（Nigel Farage）——你曾称他为近代史上最具影响力的政治人物，尽管他从未担任过国会议员，或者是科尔宾，并为工党左翼的更远端发出了声音。所以，你难道不能说，在某种程度上，尽管工党的情况看起来可能很黯淡，但一切仍未尘埃落定吗？因为目前我们正处于过山车般的局势中，我们还不太清楚究竟会驶向何方。是的，所有这些都是。我认为“意料之外，情理之中”。我认为英国脱欧之所以对认知精英（用大卫·古德哈特David Goodhart的话说）造成如此巨大的冲击，正是因为它如此出乎意料。奥威尔在我们整个播客中反复引用的那篇essay中，

74
00:48:53.000 --> 00:49:40.000
positions. So whether that's the SNP in Scotland, whether that is Nigel Farage, who you have cited as perhaps the most influential political figure in recent history, even though he's never been an MP, or Corbyn, and giving a voice to the further reaches of the Labour Party on the left. So couldn't you say that, in a sense, although things may look bleak for Labour, they're still all to play for? Because at the moment, we're on such a roller coaster, we don't quite know where we're going to get to. Yes, I think all of that. I think expect the unexpected. I think the reason Brexit was such a shock for the cognitive elite, to use a David Goodhart phrase, was because it was so unexpected. Orwell, in the essay we've cited throughout this podcast,
使用了一个短语，当普通人的声音被听到时，精英阶层会感受到来自底层的拉力。在脱欧公投期间，他们的声音当然被大声且清晰地听到了。这太令人震惊了。它将大卫·卡梅伦（David Cameron）扫出了权力宝座。它最终将鲍里斯·约翰逊（Boris Johnson）送进了唐宁街10号。这是一次民主的实践。如果我说实际上其他欧盟国家也对类似问题进行过公投，而当选民给出错误结果时，他们却被告知再去投一次票，这算不算我过于民族主义？他们确实被告知再去投一次票，这正是许多狂热的留欧派所希望的，

75
00:49:40.000 --> 00:50:23.000
uses a phrase about the elites receiving a tug from below when ordinary people are heard. And they were certainly heard loud and clear during that Brexit referendum. And it was such a shock. It swept David Cameron out of power. It brought Boris Johnson ultimately to 10 Downing Street. And it was an exercise in democracy. Am I being overly nationalist if I say that actually other European Union countries had referendums on similar issues and when the electorate delivered the wrong result, they were told to go and vote again? They were told to go and vote again, indeed, which was what a lot of ardent Romanians wanted,
不是吗？他们希望选民重新考虑，他们想要第二次公投。但是，像大卫·卡梅伦那样，就英国外交政策中如此重要、如此根本的部分——换句话说，就是欧盟成员国身份——通过单一的二元全民公投做出决定，这是一次非凡的冒险行为，不是吗？嗯，确实如此。确实是。但是，正如苏格兰公投一样，从某种意义上说，

76
00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:58.000
didn't they? They wanted the electorate to think again and they wanted the second referendum. But to decide such an important fundamental part of British foreign policy, in other words, membership of the European Union on a single binary plebiscite as David Cameron did, was an extraordinary exercise in risk-taking, wasn't it? Well, it was. It was. But again, as with the Scottish referendum, in a sense,
这些都是各政党在大选中作出的承诺。因此，你可以说，这表明英国民主正在运作，因为法拉奇显然是一个庞大选民群体的代言人，而这个群体并没有被任何主要政党所代表。然而，英国民主的作用是举行了一次公投。为了给这件事加上一层更为历史性的视角，难道没有这样一种感觉吗——每当我回顾过去这——我们在谈论什么，大概七、八年——的政治时，总觉得这一切都非常具有18世纪的风貌。它喧闹、充满活力。它通常非常粗俗，而且——各方都有不良行为。有示威活动。

77
00:50:58.000 --> 00:51:32.000
these were commitments that had been made by parties in general elections. And so therefore, you could say, well, it shows that British democracy is working because Farage was obviously the spokesman for a vast constituency of people who were not being represented by any of the major political parties. And yet British democracy served to give a referendum. Just to give a sort of more historical spin on that as well, isn't there something, I always think when you look back at the politics of the last, what are we talking, sort of seven or eight years, there's something very 18th century about it all. It's rambunctious, it's boisterous. It's often been very vulgar and very – there's been bad behavior on all sides. There have been demonstrations.
但甚至连辩论的性质，你知道，英国是例外的还是欧洲共同体的一部分？还有城镇与乡村。城镇与乡村。我的意思是，这些全都是——还有历史学家罗伯特·图姆斯（Robert Tooms）等人所谈论的气质冲突，持异见的辉格党式中产阶级，以及某种托利主义，也就是英格兰性中约翰逊博士（Dr. Johnson）和约翰·布尔（John Bull）式的一面。我的意思是，所有这些对于1760年或1770年左右的某个人来说都是一眼就能认出来的，你不觉得吗？我是这么认为的。正如对思想自由交流的感觉一样，咖啡馆以及在小酒馆和公共出版物中发生的喧闹辩论。我认为这是一个非凡的时期。汤姆是对的。这段时期动荡、喧闹、令人不安。我们已经深度两极分化。接下来会发生什么？我认为这才是问题所在。汤姆，最终我们会在苏格兰举行第二次公投吗？

78
00:51:32.000 --> 00:52:38.000
But even the nature of the arguments, you know, is Britain exceptional or is Britain part of a European community? And town and country. Town and country. I mean, these are all – and the sort of the clash of temperaments that people like Robert Toombs, the historian Robert Toombs talk about, the dissenting Whiggish sort of middle classes and the sort of Toryism, the kind of Dr. Johnson, John Bullish side of Englishness. I mean, all of that would be instantly recognizable to somebody in 1760 or 1770 or something, don't you think? I do. And as would the sense of an open exchange of ideas, the coffee houses and the boisterous debates that took place in the taverns and in the public prints. And I think it's been an extraordinary period. And Tom's right. It's been turbulent, it's been boisterous, it's been unsettling. We've been deeply polarized. And what comes next? I think that's the question. Do we have, ultimately, Tom, a second referendum
苏格兰民族问题会卷土重来并最终击碎英国国家吗？还是会被无限期推迟？我不知道。但我不认为这段动荡期已经结束。我的感觉是，如果民族主义者目前无法突破50%的得票率，老实说，我看不出他们以后还能怎么做到这一点。民调会给出答案的。时间会证明一切。多米尼克关于18世纪的观点很有趣。奥威尔在他的essay中说，1840年的英格兰与1940年的英格兰能有什么共同点？但是，你和你母亲放在壁炉架上的那张五岁孩子的照片又有什么共同点？奥威尔说，什么都没有，除了你们碰巧是同一个人。是的，我喜欢那段话。我太喜欢了。我认为这正是让我感兴趣的地方，也是我试图在书中探讨的内容，即这种“变化的永恒性”。贯穿时间的、将英格兰的现在与更深厚的

79
